@node Requirements
@subsection Requirements
@cindex requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item
Emacs 29.1 or newer.
@item
The @command{bd} executable on @var{PATH}.
@item
The MELPA libraries @code{transient} (0.10.1+) and @code{vui} (1.0.0+).
@item
@command{dolt} and @command{graphviz} for the full feature set and the
complete test suite.
@end itemize

@node Installing from MELPA
@subsection Installing from MELPA
@cindex MELPA

The package is published on MELPA:

@lisp
(use-package beads
  :ensure t)
@end lisp

@node Installing from Source
@subsection Installing from Source
@cindex installing from source

Clone the repository and add its @file{lisp/} directory to your load
path:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/beads.el/lisp")
(require 'beads)
@end lisp

@node Initial Setup
@subsection Initial Setup
@cindex initial setup
@cindex @command{bd} init

Initialize a Beads store in a project directory:

@example
cd /path/to/project
bd init
@end example

Then open the board in Emacs with @kbd{M-x beads}.  @code{beads.el}
finds the store from the buffer's @code{default-directory}; act on a
different project with the @code{:directory} argument
(@pxref{Store Scoping}).

@node Your First Issue
@section Your First Issue
@cindex first issue

@deffn Command beads
Open the Beads board for the current project.  This is the primary entry
point of @code{beads.el}; it opens the full board (@code{beads-dashboard}).
Press @kbd{?} inside the buffer for the @code{beads-dispatch} menu.
@end deffn

@enumerate
@item
Open the board: @kbd{M-x beads}.
@item
Press @kbd{?} for the dispatch menu, then @kbd{c} to create an issue.
@item
Type the title on the first line, then the description below it.
@item
Press @kbd{C-c C-c} to submit.  The new issue appears in refreshed lists.
@end enumerate

From the board, press @kbd{?} again and use:

@table @kbd
@item l
List all issues.
@item r
List ready (unblocked) work.
@item b
List blocked work.
@item D
Open the full project dashboard.
@end table

@node Interface Concepts
@section Interface Concepts
